dir
description: A plain filesystem directory - an object with keyed entries
- >
  A **directory** is a mapping: FILENAMES are the keys, and the entries are
  [blobs](::language:concretes:00-storage:02-binary_files) or nested documents - the
  [storage axis](::language:concretes:00-storage) names the concretes.
  A plain dir parses into the abstract as an OBJECT with keyed elements, and nothing
  more: it cannot BE a list - disk has no order - and it cannot carry a scalar self-value; an
  INSTANCE OVERLAY lifts both limits, and the two concretes below differ only in where that
  overlay is kept. Scalar CHILDREN are fine - a small file is a scalar:
- !!<format: text/plain> |
  julia/
    name       - "Julia"
    age        - 42
    pets/
      cat      - "Whiskers"
- >
  Case sensitivity of the keys is the FILESYSTEM's, not the language's - it belongs to the entry
  NAME, a construct of its own with the two platform concretes under it. An entry's value can be
  ANY yamlover representation - the grammar recurses here:
- *: 00-dir_yo
- *: 01-dir_index_yo
- *: 02-filename
- *..
